---
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Program = `let temps = [18, 22, 31, 27, 15];

function hottest(list)
{
  let best = list[0];

  for (let i = 1; i < list.length; i++)
  {
    if (list[i] > best)
    {
      best = list[i];
    }
  }

  return best;
}

console.log("Hottest: " + hottest(temps).toFixed(1));`;

const Prompt = `WRITTEN RESPONSE 2(b)

  Describe a change to the procedure that will
  result in a RUN-TIME error.

  AND explain why the change will result in a
  run-time error.`;

const Changes = `A   Change the call to  hottest(temps[0])

B   Change temps to an empty list:  let temps = [];

C   Change the loop to start at  i = 0  instead of  i = 1

D   Change the loop condition to  i <= list.length

E   Change the last line of hottest to  return bests;

F   Change  list[i] > best  to  list[i] < best`;

const ThreeKinds = `RUN-TIME ERROR    the program stops while running

LOGIC ERROR       the program finishes and the answer is wrong

NO EFFECT         the program finishes with the same answer`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Written Response Rehearsal: The Run-Time Error</h1>

        <h4 class="text-sn-theme">
          Six changes &middot; on paper &middot; this is Written Response 2(b)
        </h4>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <section id="objective">
          <h2>Objective</h2>

          <Code code={Prompt} frame="code" lang="txt" title="The Real Prompt" showLineNumbers={false} class="my-3" />

          <p>
            <b>Both halves again.</b> Describe the change <i>and</i> explain why it
            causes a run-time error.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>This page is in JavaScript, not exam notation, and that is
            deliberate.</b> The reference sheet defines exactly <i>two</i> ways a
            program can stop early &mdash; an out-of-range list index, and an illegal
            robot move &mdash; and nothing else. Your own program can fail in far more
            ways than that. <b class="text-sn-theme">Run-time errors are a fact about a
            language, not about an algorithm</b>, and in May you will be writing about
            your own JavaScript.
          </p>

          <p class="text-body-secondary">
            <b>Paper only. Do not run these.</b> The whole skill is predicting, and
            running it first is the one way to learn nothing.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <section id="problem">
          <h2>The Program</h2>

          <Code code={Program} frame="code" lang="javascript" title="hottest" class="mb-4" />

          <p>
            It prints <code>Hottest: 31.0</code>. <b>Six changes are proposed
            below.</b> One at a time, from the original.
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-4">Three Possible Outcomes</h3>

          <Code code={ThreeKinds} frame="code" lang="txt" title="Sort Each Change Into One" showLineNumbers={false} class="mb-4" />

          <p>
            <b>Written Response 2(b) asks for the first kind only.</b> The other two
            are here because students hand in the wrong kind, confidently, and lose
            the point.
          </p>

          <Code code={Changes} frame="code" lang="txt" title="The Six Changes" showLineNumbers={false} class="mb-4" />

          <h3 class="mt-4">For Each One</h3>

          <ol>
            <li><b>Which of the three outcomes?</b></li>
            <li><b>Why?</b> One sentence. If it is a run-time error, say what the program tries to do that it cannot.</li>
            <li>If it finishes, <b>what does it print?</b></li>
          </ol>

          <p class="mt-3">
            <b>All three outcomes appear, and no, I am not telling you how many of
            each.</b> A count would let you stop thinking once you had found them.{" "}
            <b>At least one of the six will surprise you</b>, and the one that
            surprises you is the reason this page exists.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Then: Your Own</h3>

          <p>
            <b>Find the procedure you will paste into Procedure (i) of your Project
            Reference.</b> Write out an answer to Written Response 2(b) about it
            &mdash; a change, and why it causes a run-time error.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>It must be a change to that procedure</b>, and it must genuinely stop
            the program. <b class="text-sn-theme">&quot;Change the greater-than to a
            less-than&quot; is a logic error and earns nothing.</b>
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>
            A table with a row for each of the six changes &mdash; outcome, why, and
            what it prints. Plus your own Written Response 2(b) in full sentences.
          </p>

          <p>Worth <b>5 points</b>, graded on completion.</p>
        </section>

        <h1 class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash">
          Commence Challenge
        </h1>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
